            { q: "What is Terraform state?", opts: ["The current cloud status", "Terraform's mapping of your configuration to real-world resources — tracks what exists, enabling change planning", "A configuration backup", "A deployment log"], a: 1, exp: "State is how Terraform knows what it manages. Without state, every apply would try to recreate everything. Remote state (S3, Terraform Cloud) enables team collaboration." },
            { q: "What does terraform plan do?", opts: ["Runs the infrastructure changes", "Shows what changes would be made without actually making them — a dry run for infrastructure changes", "Validates HCL syntax only", "Creates a deployment plan document"], a: 1, exp: "terraform plan compares desired state (config) to actual state (state file) and shows what would change: +create, ~modify, -destroy. Always review before applying." },
            { q: "What is a Terraform module?", opts: ["A Terraform plugin", "A reusable, composable collection of Terraform resources — encapsulates common patterns like a VPC or EKS cluster", "A provider configuration", "A state file section"], a: 1, exp: "Modules enable reuse: create a VPC module once, use it across all environments. Public modules available on Terraform Registry for common patterns." },
            { q: "Why should Terraform state be stored remotely?", opts: ["Remote state is faster", "Enables team collaboration, provides state locking to prevent concurrent modifications, and provides backup", "Required by Terraform", "Local state causes errors"], a: 1, exp: "Local state can't be shared. Remote state (S3 + DynamoDB for locking, Terraform Cloud) enables teams to collaborate safely — preventing two people from applying simultaneously." }
