            { q: "What is idempotency in Ansible?", opts: ["Running once is enough", "Ansible tasks check current state before acting — running the same playbook multiple times produces the same result without side effects", "A Python concept", "An Ansible performance feature"], a: 1, exp: "Idempotent modules: 'ensure this package is installed' — runs once installs it, runs again does nothing (already installed). Safe to re-run in CI without unintended changes." },
            { q: "What is an Ansible Role?", opts: ["A user permission in Ansible", "A reusable, organized structure for tasks, templates, handlers, and variables — enabling sharing and reuse", "An Ansible server role", "A playbook type"], a: 1, exp: "Roles follow a standard directory structure. Share via Ansible Galaxy. A 'nginx' role handles installation, configuration, and service management — reusable across projects." },
            { q: "When should you use Ansible vs Terraform?", opts: ["They're interchangeable", "Terraform for infrastructure provisioning; Ansible for configuration management — what runs on the infrastructure", "Ansible is older and being replaced", "Terraform handles everything"], a: 1, exp: "Terraform creates the server; Ansible configures it. Often used together: Terraform provisions EC2 instances, Ansible installs and configures the application stack." },
            { q: "What is an Ansible vault?", opts: ["HashiCorp Vault integration", "Ansible's built-in encryption for sensitive data in playbooks — encrypt variables, files, or entire playbooks", "A password manager", "A secret storage service"], a: 1, exp: "ansible-vault encrypts sensitive values in YAML files. Check encrypted files into version control safely. Decrypt at runtime with vault password or key file." }
