            { q: "What makes database changes risky in CI/CD?", opts: ["Databases are complex", "Database changes can be irreversible (dropping columns), affect data integrity, and can't be easily rolled back like code", "Databases are slow to change", "Regulatory requirements"], a: 1, exp: "Code rollback: git revert and deploy. Database rollback: impossible for dropped columns, difficult for data migrations. Database changes require careful forward-only migration strategies." },
            { q: "What is a database canary deployment?", opts: ["Testing database performance", "Running the new schema version alongside the old — validating the new schema handles real queries before full cutover", "A database testing environment", "A backup verification process"], a: 1, exp: "Database canary: apply new schema to a replica, route a small percentage of reads to the new schema, validate behavior with real queries before migrating production." },
            { q: "What is database drift in a DevOps context?", opts: ["Gradual query performance degradation", "When the actual database schema diverges from the version-controlled migration scripts — due to manual changes", "Data corruption over time", "Replication lag"], a: 1, exp: "Database drift occurs when someone makes manual schema changes outside the migration framework. Drift detection tools compare actual schema to expected state from migration history." }
